  <!-- bic:source=upload -->
  # Cancelled Meeting Handler

  When a meeting has been cancelled, make sure the people affected actually know, and give the user an easy way to pick back up with a follow-up — rather than letting the cancellation just be a silent calendar change.

  ## Procedure

  1. Identify the cancelled meeting(s) the user means, using the Outlook Calendar tool — either one they name directly, or by scanning a time window for events marked cancelled.
  2. Confirm who needs to be notified: the full attendee list from the cancelled event, unless the user says otherwise (e.g. "just notify the external folks").
  3. Draft a short, polite notification email using the Outlook Mail tool. State plainly that the meeting was cancelled, name the original meeting title and time, and give a one-line reason if the user provided one. Do not invent a reason if none was given.
  4. Decide whether a follow-up makes sense:
     - If the meeting had a recurring purpose or unresolved agenda items, propose 2-3 concrete follow-up time slots (use the Outlook Calendar tool to check attendee availability) so the user can pick one to send.
     - If the meeting doesn't need to be rescheduled (e.g. it was cancelled because the topic is resolved), say so instead of proposing a follow-up nobody needs.
  5. Present the notification draft and (if applicable) the proposed follow-up options to the user for approval before sending — do not send the notification or create the follow-up meeting until the user confirms, unless they explicitly asked you to just handle it.

  ## Example

  User: "The 'Vendor Sync' meeting got cancelled, let everyone know and set up a follow-up."

  - **Notification draft (to all 4 original attendees):** "Hi all — the Vendor Sync meeting originally scheduled for Thursday 3:00 PM has been cancelled. We'll follow up with a new time shortly."
  - **Proposed follow-up times:** Friday 10:00 AM, or Monday 2:00 PM (both free for all attendees).

  ## Edge cases

  - If the cancelled event has no remaining attendees to notify (e.g. it was a personal reminder), say there's no one to notify rather than sending an empty email.
  - If attendee free/busy data is incomplete, propose times based on what's available and flag the gap rather than guessing full availability.
  - Multiple cancelled meetings in one request: handle each separately with its own notification and (if needed) its own follow-up proposal.
